.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt MODIFY_LDT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm modify_ldt
.Nd read or write the local descriptor table on x86-64
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In asm/ldt.h
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_modify_ldt" "int func" "void *ptr" "unsigned long bytecount"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads or writes the local descriptor table, the LDT, of the calling
process's address space.
It is the x86-64 kernel's call, number 154 for a 64-bit program and 123
for an i386 one; the i686 kernel does not have it.
Musl declares no function for it; use
.Fn syscall .
.Pp
The LDT holds up to 8192 descriptors of 8 bytes, which a program loads by
selectors whose bit 2, TI, is set: entry
.Va n
at RPL 3 is
.Li "(n << 3) | 7" .
Every thread of the address space shares it, as on Linux;
.Xr fork 2
gives the child a copy, in which a write changes nothing of the parent's,
and
.Xr execve 2
starts the new program with none.
Each processor loads the table of the thread it runs.
.Fa func
selects the operation:
.Bl -tag -width "0x11"
.It 0
Copy the entries up to the highest ever written to
.Fa ptr ,
then zeros, to
.Fa bytecount
bytes, at most 65536; the result is that count, or 0 when nothing was
ever written.
.It 1
Write one entry in the old mode, from the
.Vt struct user_desc
at
.Fa ptr ,
whose size
.Fa bytecount
must be, 16 bytes, as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
describes it.
A base and a limit of 0 clear the entry, and the descriptor's AVL bit is
cleared whatever
.Va useable
says.
.It 2
Copy
.Fa bytecount
zero bytes to
.Fa ptr ,
at most 128, the default LDT of Linux's 64-bit kernel; the result is that
count.
.It 0x11
Write one entry as function 1 does, keeping
.Va useable
in the AVL bit.
.El
.Pp
A write of the
.Li LDT_empty
form, read_exec_only and seg_not_present set and every other field 0,
clears the entry.
Otherwise the entry gets the descriptor Linux's
.Fn fill_ldt
makes: DPL 3, accessed, writable or readable unless read_exec_only, present
unless seg_not_present, and never a 64-bit code segment.
16-bit segments are taken, as a Linux built with
.Dv CONFIG_X86_16BIT
takes them.
A write changes the descriptor in place; DS and ES of the calling thread
that hold a selector of the LDT are loaded again, or with the null selector
where theirs no longer loads, as on Linux.
A segment register of another thread keeps the descriptor it loaded until
it loads the selector again, as the switch does.
.Pp
A switch loads the incoming thread's DS, ES, FS and GS again, and one whose
entry no longer loads gets the null selector, as Linux's fault fixup gives
it.
A return to user mode whose CS or SS no longer loads, or whose address is
past its code segment's limit, is the program's fault at that address: a
SIGSEGV for a general protection fault and a SIGBUS for a segment not
present or a stack fault, as on Linux.
A processor running another thread of the address space when its first
write makes the table loads it at that thread's first general protection
fault on a selector of the LDT.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The count of bytes read, 0 for a write, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
As on Linux, the result is an
.Vt int
zero-extended into the register: a 64-bit caller of
.Fn syscall
from a C library that judges the whole register finds an error as a
large positive count, and should test the result as an
.Vt int .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A write's
.Fa bytecount
is not 16, its entry is 8192 or more, or it is a conforming code segment,
contents 3, in the old mode or present.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa ptr
cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The first write found no memory for the table.
.It Bq Er ENOSYS
.Fa func
is none of the four.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arch_prctl 2 ,
.Xr set_thread_area 2 ,
.Xr ia32 7
.Sh STANDARDS
Linux's x86 call, with the same numbers and functions.
Linux's 64-bit kernel hides the high half of ESP after a return to a
16-bit stack segment behind its espfix stacks; this kernel leaves there
the high half of its own stack pointer, which the processor does not
restore.
Linux loads a table its first write makes on every processor at once, by
an interprocessor interrupt; this kernel loads it at the first fault that
needs it.
